WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.827
Molecular modeling in quantum mechanics demands
models that respect physical laws,

00:00:05.907 --> 00:00:11.433
namely invariance and equivariance to rotation and
translation of atomic coordinates.

00:00:11.513 --> 00:00:13.609
This paper introduces GeoMFormer,

00:00:13.689 --> 00:00:19.939
a general and flexible Transformer-based
architecture that learns both invariant and equivariant

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:22.181
molecular representations at once.

00:00:22.261 --> 00:00:27.325
It uses two parallel Transformer streams, one for
each type of representation,

00:00:27.405 --> 00:00:34.051
and bridges them with carefully designed
cross-attention modules so information flows between the two.

00:00:34.131 --> 00:00:38.997
Many prior geometric models turn out to be special
cases of this framework,

00:00:39.077 --> 00:00:44.800
and GeoMFormer sets new state-of-the-art results
across a wide range of molecular tasks.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.015
Deep learning has become a powerful tool for
molecular science,

00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:55.602
predicting properties of molecules from their
three-dimensional coordinates and simulating how atoms move.

00:00:55.682 --> 00:00:58.771
But these tasks impose strict physical constraints.

00:00:58.851 --> 00:01:05.234
A model's prediction must transform correctly when
the input coordinate system is rotated or translated,

00:01:05.314 --> 00:01:11.137
a requirement known as invariance for scalar
quantities and equivariance for vector quantities.

00:01:11.217 --> 00:01:13.747
Existing methods handle these constraints,

00:01:13.827 --> 00:01:16.916
but most are built on heuristic and costly modules,

00:01:16.996 --> 00:01:23.068
and few offer a single general framework that
learns both invariant and equivariant representations

00:01:23.148 --> 00:01:24.870
effectively at the same time.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.290
The trouble with existing geometric models is that
they are largely built by hand.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:33.410
Designers craft specialized equivariant modules
that are either expensive to scale or

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:37.530
so constrained that they sacrifice expressive power,

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.650
and the resulting architectures grow complex just
to guarantee the physical constraints.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:45.770
More importantly,

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.890
real applications increasingly demand a single
model that performs both invariant and

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:54.010
equivariant prediction with strong accuracy.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:58.130
There is a clear need for a general, flexible
framework built on well-understood,

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:02.250
standard components rather than one-off heuristic modules.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:06.440
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.330
First, it introduces GeoMFormer,

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:14.220
a novel Transformer-based molecular model that
maintains two separate streams,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.110
one for invariant and one for equivariant representations,

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:22.000
using only standard Transformer building blocks.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.890
Second, it designs cross-attention modules that
bridge these two streams,

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.780
letting each draw on contextual information from
the other to enhance geometric modeling.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.670
Third,

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.560
it shows that this framework is general enough that
many previously proposed architectures

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.450
can be viewed as special instantiations of GeoMFormer,

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.340
and it backs the design with strong empirical
results across a diverse set of molecular tasks.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.362
GeoMFormer keeps two representations for every
atom: an invariant feature vector and an

00:02:51.442 --> 00:02:53.861
equivariant three-dimensional feature.

00:02:53.941 --> 00:02:57.281
These flow through two parallel Transformer streams.

00:02:57.361 --> 00:03:02.609
Within each stream, a self-attention module first
mixes information across atoms.

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:04.385
For the equivariant stream,

00:03:04.465 --> 00:03:10.238
standard attention is modified so the attention
score is computed by summing dot products

00:03:10.318 --> 00:03:13.461
over the three-dimensional Query and Key vectors,

00:03:13.541 --> 00:03:15.960
which provably preserves equivariance.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.605
Then, the key innovation,

00:03:17.685 --> 00:03:23.327
a cross-attention module lets each stream query the
other: the invariant stream attends

00:03:23.407 --> 00:03:26.023
to the equivariant stream and vice versa,

00:03:26.103 --> 00:03:29.049
fusing the two kinds of geometric information.

00:03:29.129 --> 00:03:33.521
A feed-forward network completes each block, and
blocks are stacked.

00:03:33.601 --> 00:03:38.717
Because the design uses only standard Transformer
components arranged this way,

00:03:38.797 --> 00:03:43.650
many earlier geometric networks fall out as special
cases of the framework.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:49.897
The authors evaluate GeoMFormer across a broad
suite of tasks that together stress both

00:03:49.977 --> 00:03:52.357
invariant and equivariant abilities.

00:03:52.437 --> 00:03:54.681
On the Open Catalyst 2020 dataset,

00:03:54.761 --> 00:03:59.534
spanning over four hundred sixty thousand
adsorbate-catalyst complexes,

00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:05.002
they test both the Initial Structure to Relaxed
Energy task, which is invariant,

00:04:05.082 --> 00:04:10.060
and the Initial Structure to Relaxed Structure
task, which is equivariant.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.434
On the large quantum chemistry datasets PCQM4Mv2
and Molecule3D,

00:04:14.514 --> 00:04:18.946
with millions of molecules, they predict the
HOMO-LUMO energy gap.

00:04:19.026 --> 00:04:25.849
They further use a synthetic five-particle N-body
simulation to test equivariant position prediction,

00:04:25.929 --> 00:04:30.633
and the MD17 dataset for force-field modeling in
the ablation studies.

00:04:30.713 --> 00:04:36.580
This breadth lets a single architecture be judged
on both scalar and vector prediction.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.759
GeoMFormer delivers strong results everywhere it is
tested.

00:04:40.839 --> 00:04:46.149
On the Open Catalyst energy prediction task it
outperforms prior invariant models,

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:50.817
and on the structure prediction task it excels at
equivariant modeling.

00:04:50.897 --> 00:04:56.930
On the PCQM4Mv2 benchmark it reaches the lowest
error among models with quadratic complexity,

00:04:57.010 --> 00:05:01.401
a six point seven percent relative reduction over
the previous best,

00:05:01.481 --> 00:05:05.148
while staying efficient enough to scale to large systems.

00:05:05.228 --> 00:05:10.801
On Molecule3D it improves error by sixteen point
three percent on the random split and

00:05:10.881 --> 00:05:13.891
eleven point six percent on the scaffold split.

00:05:13.971 --> 00:05:20.662
And on the N-body simulation it cuts mean squared
error by a striking thirty-three point eight percent.

00:05:20.742 --> 00:05:24.672
A single architecture, built from standard
Transformer parts,

00:05:24.752 --> 00:05:29.800
achieves state-of-the-art performance on both
invariant and equivariant tasks.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:35.186
To understand where the gains come from, the
authors ablate each building block.

00:05:35.266 --> 00:05:42.307
The most telling finding concerns the
cross-attention modules that bridge the invariant and equivariant streams.

00:05:42.387 --> 00:05:44.087
Removing them hurts sharply.

00:05:44.167 --> 00:05:46.313
On the MD17 energy prediction task,

00:05:46.393 --> 00:05:52.416
adding the invariant cross-attention gives an
eighteen point seven percent relative improvement,

00:05:52.496 --> 00:05:56.422
the equivariant cross-attention gives nine point
eight percent,

00:05:56.502 --> 00:06:00.046
and using both together gives twenty point eight percent.

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:04.305
On the harder MD17 force prediction task the effect
is even larger,

00:06:04.385 --> 00:06:10.473
with a sixty point eight percent relative
improvement when both cross-attention modules are used.

00:06:10.553 --> 00:06:15.686
On the N-body simulation the combined improvement
is seventeen point five percent.

00:06:15.766 --> 00:06:17.784
The self-attention, feed-forward,

00:06:17.864 --> 00:06:21.154
and layer-normalization modules also each contribute,

00:06:21.234 --> 00:06:25.350
but the cross-attention bridge is clearly the heart
of the design.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:29.510
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:33.370
On PCQM4Mv2,

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:37.230
GeoMFormer reaches a validation error of zero point
zero seven three four,

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:41.090
the best of any quadratic-complexity model,

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.950
a six point seven percent relative reduction.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:48.810
On the N-body simulation it achieves a mean squared
error of zero point zero zero four seven,

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:52.670
a thirty-three point eight percent reduction over
the previous best.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.530
On Molecule3D it records errors of zero point zero
two five two on the random split and

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:00.390
zero point one zero four five on the scaffold split,

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:04.250
improvements of sixteen point three and eleven
point six percent.

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:08.110
And in the ablations,

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:11.970
adding cross-attention yields up to a sixty point
eight percent relative improvement on force prediction,

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.830
underscoring how central that bridge is.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:20.250
The lasting message of this work is that you do not
need bespoke,

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:24.307
heuristic modules to model molecules under physical
constraints.

00:07:24.387 --> 00:07:27.856
By running two standard Transformer streams in parallel,

00:07:27.936 --> 00:07:31.089
one for invariant and one for equivariant features,

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:34.131
and connecting them with simple cross-attention,

00:07:34.211 --> 00:07:40.216
GeoMFormer learns both kinds of representation at
once and outperforms specialized architectures

00:07:40.296 --> 00:07:41.801
on a wide range of tasks.

00:07:41.881 --> 00:07:45.858
Because many earlier models are special cases of
this framework,

00:07:45.938 --> 00:07:48.076
GeoMFormer offers a clean, general,

00:07:48.156 --> 00:07:53.020
and scalable design principle for geometric
molecular representation learning.
